### Instruction:
Construct a regex in Java that matches a valid email address and also validates the domain name.

### Response:
To construct a regex in Java that matches a valid email address and validates the domain name, you can use the following pattern:

```java
String emailRegex = "^[a-zA-Z0-9_+&*-]+(?:\\.[a-zA-Z0-9_+&*-]+)*@(?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]+\\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,7}$";
```

This regex pattern consists of the following parts:

- `^` - Beginning of the string
- `[a-zA-Z0-9_+&*-]+` - One or more alphanumeric characters, underscore, plus sign, ampersand, asterisk, or hyphen
- `(?:\\.[a-zA-Z0-9_+&*-]+)*` - A non-capturing group for matching zero or more occurrences of a dot followed by one or more alphanumeric characters, underscore, plus sign, ampersand, asterisk, or hyphen (for domain extensions like .co.uk)
- `@` - The "@" symbol
- `(?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]+\\.)+` - A non-capturing group for matching one or more occurrences of one or more alphanumeric characters or hyphen followed by a dot (for subdomains)
- `[a-zA-Z]{2,7}` - Two to seven alphabetic characters (for top-level domain)
- `$` - End of the string

Here's an example usage:

```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;

public class EmailValidation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String emailRegex = "^[a-zA-Z0-9_+&*-]+(?:\\.[a-zA-Z0-9_+&*-]+)*@(?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]+\\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,7}$";
        String[] emails = {"example@example.com", "example@example", "example@123.45", "example@domain.co.uk"};

        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(emailRegex);

        for (String email : emails) {
            Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(email);
            if (matcher.matches()) {
                System.out.println(email + " is a valid email address.");
            } else {
                System.out.println(email + " is not a valid email address.");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This example will print:

```
example@example.com is a valid email address.
example@example is not a valid email address.
example@123.45 is not a valid email address.
example@domain.co.uk is a valid email address.
```

Note that this regex pattern is a basic implementation and might not cover all possible valid email address formats.

