      />
      <input
        type="text"
        placeholder="Phone Number"
        value={phoneNumber}
        onChange={(e) => {
          setPhoneNumber(e.target.value);
          validateForm();
        }}
      />
      <button type="submit" disabled={!isValidForm}>
        Submit
      </button>
    </form>
  );
};

export default Form;
```

In this example, we use the `useState` hook to manage the state of each input field. The `validateEmail`, `validatePassword`, and `validatePhoneNumber` functions are used to perform the validation logic for each respective field. The `handleFormSubmit` function is called when the form is submitted and can be used to perform any necessary form submission logic. The `validateForm` function is called whenever any input field changes in order to update the `isValidForm` state.

Each input field has an `onChange` event handler that updates the corresponding state value and then calls the `validateForm` function. The `disabled` attribute on the submit button is set to `!isValidForm`, which means it will be disabled if `isValidForm` is false.

Note that the time complexity for validating the email, password, confirm password, and phone number fields is O(1) because the regular expressions used for validation have a fixed number of operations. The time complexity for validating the remaining fields is O(n) because we check if their values are empty. The space complexity is O(1) because we are not using any additional data structures or storing the input values in arrays or objects.

