Modify the code to make sure it is valid according to Java language specification. The program should take two user inputs as command line arguments and print them along with "Hello World!". Additionally, the program should check if the second input is a valid email address before printing it. If the second input is not a valid email address, the program should print an error message. The program should also verify that the first input is a valid phone number in the format (XXX) XXX-XXXX, where X represents a digit. If the first input is not a valid phone number, the program should print an error message.

### Response:
Here is the modified code that ensures it is valid according to the Java language specification:

```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class HelloWorld {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length != 2) {
            System.out.println("Please provide two arguments: phone number and email address.");
            return;
        }

        String phoneNumber = args[0];
        String email = args[1];

        if (!isValidPhoneNumber(phoneNumber)) {
            System.out.println("Invalid phone number format. Please enter a valid phone number in the format (XXX) XXX-XXXX.");
            return;
        }

        if (!isValidEmail(email)) {
            System.out.println("Invalid email address format. Please enter a valid email address.");
            return;
        }

        System.out.println(phoneNumber);
        System.out.println(email);
        System.out.println("Hello World!");
    }

    private static boolean isValidPhoneNumber(String phoneNumber) {
        String pattern = "^\\(\\d{3}\\) \\d{3}-\\d{4}$";
        return Pattern.matches(pattern, phoneNumber);
    }

    private static boolean isValidEmail(String email) {
        String pattern = "^[A-Za-z0-9+_.-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+$";
        return Pattern.matches(pattern, email);
    }
